\section*{Abstract}

High-dimensional regression with highly correlated predictors is a challenging problem because strong multicollinearity may lead to unstable coefficient estimation, unreliable variable selection, and excessive shrinkage of important signals. Although the TPBN-MBSP model provides an effective Bayesian global-local shrinkage framework for sparse high-dimensional regression, its original prior structure does not explicitly incorporate the correlation information among predictors. To address this limitation, this thesis proposes a modified TPBN-MBSP method by introducing a correlation-aware prior covariance structure for the regression coefficients.

The proposed method constructs a predictor graph from the empirical correlation matrix and uses the corresponding graph Laplacian matrix to incorporate dependence information into the coefficient prior. Specifically, the prior covariance matrix is modified as 
\[
\Sigma_\beta = D_\psi R_\beta D_\psi + \phi L,
\]
where \(D_\psi\) retains the local shrinkage structure, \(R_\beta\) captures coefficient-level correlation, and \(L\) reflects the graph structure among highly correlated predictors. The onion method is also used to generate valid positive definite correlation matrices during posterior computation.

Simulation studies under different correlation settings show that the proposed method is especially useful when predictors are strongly or nearly perfectly correlated. Compared with the original TPBN-MBSP model, the proposed method can reduce false signals among correlated noise variables and improve estimation stability under severe multicollinearity. Real data analyses using diamond price data and house price data further demonstrate that the proposed method maintains competitive predictive performance while providing a more flexible and correlation-aware variable selection framework. Overall, the proposed method can be viewed as a structured extension of TPBN-MBSP for high-dimensional regression problems involving highly correlated predictors.

