\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

High-dimensional regression with strongly correlated predictors arises routinely in modern applications such as genomics, imaging, and finance. In such settings, the number of predictors $p$ can exceed the sample size $n$, while only a small subset of predictors is expected to be truly associated with the response(s). A widely used Bayesian strategy for sparse learning is to adopt \emph{global--local} (GL) shrinkage priors, which combine substantial prior mass near zero with heavy tails to avoid over-shrinking large signals. This behavior makes GL priors attractive for inducing sparsity and stabilizing estimation in high dimensions. In the multivariate linear regression model
\begin{equation}
Y = X B + E,
\qquad E_i \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} N_q(0,\Sigma),
\label{eq:mvr}
\end{equation}
Bai and Ghosh (2018) proposed the \emph{Multivariate Bayesian model with Shrinkage Priors} (MBSP), which places a matrix-normal GL prior on the coefficient matrix $B$ to encourage row-wise sparsity and enable variable selection in multivariate regression. In particular, MBSP is formulated via a matrix-normal likelihood and a matrix-normal prior on $B$ with row-specific local scales, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
Y \mid X,B,\Sigma \sim MN_{n\times q}(XB, I_n, \Sigma),
B \mid \xi_1,\ldots,\xi_p,\Sigma \sim MN_{p\times q}\!\big(0,\ \tau\,\mathrm{diag}(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_p),\ \Sigma\big)
\label{eq:mbsp_core}
\end{equation}
with $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_p$ following a polynomial-tailed GL prior.  %
Theoretical results in \cite{BaiGhosh2018} further provide sufficient conditions under which the posterior can be consistent even when $p>n$ and $p$ grows nearly exponentially with $n$.

A particularly convenient and widely used GL family is the \emph{three-parameter beta normal} (TPBN) family. Bai and Ghosh (2018) show how MBSP can be implemented using TPBN as a special case and describe a fully Bayesian MBSP-TPBN specification with conjugate components that admit closed-form full conditionals for Gibbs sampling. %
In the TPBN hierarchy, each coefficient (or each row-scale in the multivariate case) is endowed with a local scale parameter governed by a heavy-tailed mixing distribution; several popular priors (e.g., the horseshoe) appear as special cases.  %
These developments make TPBN-MBSP an appealing baseline for sparse multivariate learning in large $p$ problems.

Despite these strengths, a recurring practical challenge in high-dimensional regression is that \emph{strong predictor correlation} can degrade the stability of shrinkage-based learning and variable selection. When many predictors are nearly collinear, information about the signal can be spread across correlated features, and posterior shrinkage may concentrate on arbitrary representatives within a correlated group. This phenomenon can manifest as unstable selection across runs, inflated posterior uncertainty within highly correlated blocks, and reduced predictive performance when the fitted model fails to borrow strength across correlated predictors. While TPBN-MBSP is designed for sparsity and scalability, it does not, by default, encode explicit structure among predictors beyond the shrinkage hierarchy. Consequently, in settings dominated by high correlation, additional structure is desirable so that the prior can encourage \emph{coherent behavior} (e.g., smoothing or grouping) among coefficients attached to strongly related predictors.

The main goal of this thesis is to improve TPBN-MBSP in the presence of high predictor correlation by introducing a \emph{graph-structured} dependence component into the prior for regression coefficients. Specifically, we construct a weighted graph on predictors using a thresholded empirical correlation matrix and form the corresponding (scaled) graph Laplacian $L$. The Laplacian is then incorporated into the coefficient prior through a structured covariance component. In the univariate case (for clarity of exposition), letting $\beta\in\mathbb{R}^p$ denote the coefficient vector, our Version~2 algorithm modifies the prior covariance to
\begin{equation}
\Sigma_\beta \;=\; D_{\psi}\,R_{\beta}\,D_{\psi} \;+\; \phi\,L,
\label{eq:version2_sigbeta_intro}
\end{equation}
where $D_{\psi}=\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{\psi_1},\ldots,\sqrt{\psi_p})$ collects TPBN-style local shrinkage parameters, $R_{\beta}$ is a correlation matrix governing residual dependence among coefficients, and $\phi>0$ controls the strength of the Laplacian contribution. This construction preserves the global--local shrinkage mechanism (via $\psi_j$) while injecting a graph-based smoothing/grouping effect (via $L$), thereby explicitly targeting the core difficulty of highly correlated predictors. In the resulting posterior computation, every update involving $\Sigma_\beta$ (e.g., $\beta$, $\sigma^2$, and Metropolis steps for local scales and correlation parameters) automatically propagates the Laplacian structure, enabling the sampler to share information across correlated features rather than treating them as exchangeable a priori.

A second computational ingredient in our Version~2 algorithm is an efficient mechanism for sampling (or proposing) correlation matrices for $R_{\beta}$. We adopt the \emph{onion method} of Lewandowski, Kurowicka, and Joe (2009), which generates random correlation matrices by sequentially augmenting dimension while maintaining positive definiteness by construction.
The onion method exploits a block partition identity and a determinant recursion for correlation matrices, and it yields an explicit algorithm based on Beta draws and uniform samples on hyperspheres; practical implementation can use the Cholesky decomposition for numerical efficiency. 
In our MCMC implementation, onion-based proposals provide a principled LKJ-type independence proposal for $R_\beta$, simplifying acceptance ratios and helping avoid the numerical difficulties that can arise from naive random-walk proposals in high dimension.

In summary, this thesis focuses on the high-correlation regime where standard shrinkage approaches can exhibit weaker empirical performance. Building upon TPBN-MBSP and its efficient Gibbs sampling framework, we develop a Version~2 algorithm that incorporates predictor-graph information through a Laplacian-augmented prior covariance and leverages onion-based correlation matrix generation for stable posterior computation.
The remainder of the thesis is organized as follows. Chapter~2 reviews development and applications of regression methods for high-dimensional or highly correlated data, as well as TPBN, graph Laplacian theory, and the onion method. Chapter~3 introduces method we propose and details the MCMC algorithm. Chapter~4 evaluates the proposed method through simulation studies designed to emphasize strong predictor correlation, and Chapter~5 presents an application to real high-dimensional data with pronounced correlation structure. Chapter~6 discuss the future work and our conclusions.



