\section{Literature Review}
High-dimensional regression with \emph{highly correlated predictors} poses two intertwined challenges: (i) \textbf{statistical indentifiability and stability} (many coefficient vectors can fit nearly equally well under collinearity), and (ii) \textbf{interpretability under redundancy} (selection prededures may arbitrarily choose one variable among a correlated cluster). In this chapter's literature review, previous methods for handling high-dimensional and highly correlated data will first be discussed. Then, according to the methods adopted in this study, the applications of \textbf{graph Laplacian theory}, \textbf{Bayesian global-local shrinkage}, and the \textbf{onion method} in regression analysis will be introduced in sequence.

\subsection{High-Dimensional Regression with Highly Correlated Predictors}
Consider linear regression $y = X\beta^\star + \varepsilon$ with correlated columns of $X$. Near-collinearity implies that distinct sparse vectors can yield similar predictions, making selection unstable and rendering certain lasso-style sufficient conditions stringent. This motivated penalties ans priors that encourage grouping effects, fuse coefficients along correlation structure, or explicitly encode dependence graphs.

\subsubsection{Correlation Stabilization and Grouping via Mixed Norms}

Elastic Net combines the $\ell_1$ lasso penalty with an $\ell_2$ ``ridge'' penalty to improve stability when predictors are highly correlated, yielding a grouping effect in which strongly correlated variables tend to enter or leave the model together. Consider the standard linear regression setting
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. In this framework, the elastic net estimator is obtained by solving
\[
\hat{\beta}^{EN}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2n}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_1 \|\beta\|_1
+
\lambda_2 \|\beta\|_2^2
\right\},
\]
where
\[
\|\beta\|_1=\sum_{j=1}^p |\beta_j|,
\qquad
\|\beta\|_2^2=\sum_{j=1}^p \beta_j^2.
\]
Here, the $\ell_1$ term encourages sparsity and performs variable selection, whereas the $\ell_2$ term shrinks correlated coefficients toward each other and improves numerical stability when predictors are highly collinear.

Zou and Hastie (2005) introduced the elastic net for high-dimensional settings (where $p \gg n$), showing that the added $\ell_2$ term helps mitigate the instability of the lasso under correlated designs and presenting an algorithm (LARS-EN) to compute the solution path. Their formulation is often written in the equivalent mixed-penalty form
\[
\hat{\beta}^{EN}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2n}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda
\left(
\alpha \|\beta\|_1
+
\frac{1-\alpha}{2}\|\beta\|_2^2
\right)
\right\},
\]
where $\lambda > 0$ controls the overall amount of regularization and $\alpha \in [0,1]$ determines the balance between the two penalties. In particular, $\alpha=1$ corresponds to the lasso, $\alpha=0$ corresponds to ridge regression, and $0<\alpha<1$ yields the elastic net. This representation makes clear how the method simultaneously achieves shrinkage and variable selection. Moreover, when two predictors are strongly correlated, the elastic net tends to assign them similar estimated coefficients, reflecting the grouping effect, that is,
\[
x_j \approx x_k
\quad \Longrightarrow \quad
\hat{\beta}^{EN}_j \approx \hat{\beta}^{EN}_k.
\]

This formulation has been widely adopted in practice: Friedman, Hastie, and Tibshirani (2010) developed fast coordinate-descent routines (the \texttt{glmnet} package) to fit elastic-net-penalized linear and generalized linear models efficiently. As a result, elastic net regularization is routinely used in applications such as genomics and other ``large $p$, small $n$'' problems, allowing one to fit linear, logistic, Poisson, and Cox regression models while automatically handling groups of correlated predictors and providing more stable estimates than the pure lasso.

\subsubsection{Clustering and Group Sparsity for Correlated Predictors}

For regression problems involving highly correlated predictors, an important line of research has focused on methods that combine sparsity with clustering or groupwise structure in order to alleviate the instability of variable selection under multicollinearity. Consider the standard linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the coefficient vector. In this context, Bondell and Reich (2008) proposed OSCAR, a regularization method that combines an $\ell_1$ penalty with a pairwise penalty encouraging certain regression coefficients to be exactly equal, thereby achieving simultaneous variable selection and supervised clustering of predictors. The OSCAR estimator can be written as
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\text{OSCAR}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_1 \sum_{j=1}^p |\beta_j|
+
\lambda_2 \sum_{j<k}\max\bigl(|\beta_j|,|\beta_k|\bigr)
\right\},
\]
where the $\ell_1$ term encourages sparsity and the pairwise $\max(\cdot,\cdot)$ penalty promotes equality among some coefficients. This formulation is particularly well suited to high-dimensional settings with correlated covariates, since redundant predictors can be assigned identical coefficients rather than being selected in an arbitrary ``pick-one'' manner, thus improving interpretability and stability.

A related but distinct direction was developed by Yuan and Lin (2006), who introduced the group lasso framework for regression models with grouped variables. Suppose that the predictors are partitioned into $G$ predefined groups, and let $\beta_g$ denote the subvector of coefficients corresponding to group $g$. By imposing penalties based on group norms, the group lasso estimator is defined as
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\text{Group}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda \sum_{g=1}^G w_g \|\beta_g\|_2
\right\},
\]
where $w_g$ is a group-specific weight, often chosen as $\sqrt{p_g}$ with $p_g$ denoting the size of group $g$. Because the penalty is imposed on the entire subvectors $\beta_g$, group lasso performs selection at the level of whole groups and is therefore especially useful when correlation among predictors is aligned with known structural groupings, such as basis expansions, categorical factors, or biological pathways. This framework was later extended by Meier, van de Geer, and B\"uhlmann (2008) to logistic regression, where the group lasso was shown to provide an efficient and effective approach for high-dimensional generalized linear models with grouped predictors, transferring the advantages of groupwise stabilization to classification problems.

However, because group lasso selects or discards entire groups, it may be overly restrictive when only a subset of variables within a relevant group contributes to the response. To address this limitation, Simon, Friedman, Hastie, and Tibshirani (2013) proposed the sparse-group lasso, which combines the group lasso penalty with the standard lasso penalty to enforce both group-level sparsity and within-group sparsity. Its estimator takes the form
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\text{SGL}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
(1-\alpha)\lambda \sum_{g=1}^G w_g \|\beta_g\|_2
+
\alpha\lambda \|\beta\|_1
\right\},
\]
where $\alpha \in [0,1]$ controls the balance between groupwise and elementwise sparsity. When $\alpha=0$, the method reduces to the group lasso, whereas larger values of $\alpha$ allow additional sparsity within selected groups. This hybrid regularization scheme is particularly attractive in modern high-dimensional applications, such as multi-omics analysis and functional regression, where predictors often exhibit meaningful grouping structures but only a portion of the variables within selected groups are truly informative.

Taken together, these methods demonstrate that in the presence of strong predictor correlation, regression performance can be substantially improved by moving beyond purely elementwise sparsity and incorporating either coefficient clustering or structured groupwise regularization into the estimation procedure.



\subsubsection{Fusion/TV/Generalized Lasso for Structured Correlation}

In regression settings where predictors exhibit structured local dependence, an important class of methods has emerged around fusion-based and total-variation-type penalties. Consider the standard regression framework
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the coefficient vector. Tibshirani, Saunders, Rosset, Zhu, and Knight (2005) introduced the fused lasso, which augments the standard $\ell_1$ penalty on regression coefficients with an additional $\ell_1$ penalty on successive differences between adjacent coefficients. The fused lasso estimator can be written as
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\text{FL}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_1 \sum_{j=1}^p |\beta_j|
+
\lambda_2 \sum_{j=2}^p |\beta_j-\beta_{j-1}|
\right\}.
\]
This formulation yields estimates that are simultaneously sparse and piecewise constant, making it especially suitable for ordered predictors, such as those indexed by time, space, or genomic location, where neighboring variables are often highly correlated and expected to exert similar effects. By encouraging adjacent coefficients to be equal through the fusion penalty
\[
\sum_{j=2}^p |\beta_j-\beta_{j-1}|,
\]
the fused lasso stabilizes estimation across correlated neighborhoods while still permitting abrupt changes when supported by the data.

This line of work was substantially generalized by Tibshirani and Taylor (2011), who proposed the generalized lasso framework, in which the successive-difference operator is replaced by a general linear operator $D$ and the penalty takes the form
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\text{GL}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\ell(\beta)+\lambda \|D\beta\|_1
\right\},
\]
where $\ell(\beta)$ is a convex loss function, such as the squared-error loss
\[
\ell(\beta)=\frac{1}{2}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2.
\]
When $D$ is chosen as the first-difference matrix
\[
D=
\begin{pmatrix}
-1 & 1 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & -1 & 1 & \cdots & 0\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
0 & 0 & 0 & \cdots & 1
\end{pmatrix},
\]
the generalized lasso reduces to the fused lasso. More generally, this extension allows fusion and total variation regularization to be imposed on arbitrary graph structures rather than only on ordered sequences, thereby providing a flexible mechanism for incorporating known adjacency relations or network topology directly into the estimation procedure. Their contribution also established a dual path algorithm and an unbiased estimator of degrees of freedom, thereby strengthening the theoretical and computational foundations of structured $\ell_1$ regularization.

Related developments further clarified the statistical properties of graph-based fusion methods. Sharpnack, Singh, and Rinaldo (2012), for example, studied the edge lasso on general graphs, where the $\ell_1$ norm of coefficient differences across graph edges is penalized in order to recover piecewise constant signals over graph domains. Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph with vertex set $V$ and edge set $E$. Then the graph-based fusion penalty can be expressed as
\[
\sum_{(u,v)\in E} |\beta_u-\beta_v|
\;=\;
\|D_G \beta\|_1,
\]
where $D_G$ is the incidence matrix of the graph. Their analysis showed that, under suitable graph-structured assumptions, sparsistency and support recovery can be achieved, thereby providing theoretical justification for interpreting local constancy as a manifestation of correlation structure.

Extending these ideas beyond piecewise constant estimation, Wang, Sharpnack, Smola, and Tibshirani (2016) developed graph trend filtering, which generalized univariate trend filtering to graph settings by penalizing higher-order graph differences. A representative form of the estimator is
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\text{GTF}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2}\|y-\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda \|\Delta^{(k+1)}_G \beta\|_1
\right\},
\]
where $\Delta^{(k+1)}_G$ denotes a higher-order graph difference operator. This approach produces locally adaptive estimators that smooth over correlated regions while preserving sharp transitions, and is particularly attractive for nonparametric regression and denoising problems defined on irregular spatial or network domains.

Taken together, these studies demonstrate that fusion, total variation, and generalized lasso methods provide a coherent framework for handling highly correlated predictors when the correlation structure is structured by ordering, adjacency, or network relations. These methods improve interpretability and statistical stability by explicitly regularizing differences among structurally related coefficients.

\subsubsection{Graph-Based Regularization Tailored to Highly Correlated Designs}

A more recent strand of the literature has considered regression settings in which predictor dependence is so strong that classical assumptions, such as restricted eigenvalue or related compatibility conditions, are no longer realistic. Consider the linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. Within this context, Li, Mark, Raskutti, Willett, Song, and Neiman (2020) developed a graph-based regularization framework specifically designed for highly correlated designs by assuming that predictor covariance and regression coefficient similarity are governed by a common graph structure. In their formulation, a covariance graph is constructed using either empirical covariances or external side information, and graph total variation regularization is imposed to encourage similar regression coefficients among strongly connected or highly correlated features. Specifically, letting $\widehat{\Sigma}$ denote an estimate of the predictor covariance matrix, they define a weighted edge-incidence operator $\Gamma$ based on the covariance graph, and consider an estimator of the form
\[
\hat{\beta}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{n}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_S \|\Gamma \beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_1\left(
\lambda_{TV}\|\Gamma \beta\|_1
+
\|\beta\|_1
\right)
\right\}.
\]
Here, the graph total variation term $\|\Gamma\beta\|_1$ promotes similarity among coefficients associated with strongly connected predictors, while the quadratic term $\|\Gamma\beta\|_2^2$ acts as a graph-based smoothing or Laplacian penalty that helps stabilize estimation when the design is highly ill-conditioned. The additional $\|\beta\|_1$ term retains elementwise sparsity. In particular, for an edge $(j,k)$ in the covariance graph, the corresponding row of $\Gamma$ is constructed as
\[
|\widehat{\Sigma}_{jk}|^{1/2}
\bigl(
e_j-\operatorname{sign}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{jk})e_k
\bigr)^\top,
\]
so that
\[
\|\Gamma\beta\|_1
=
\sum_{(j,k)\in E}
|\widehat{\Sigma}_{jk}|^{1/2}
\left|
\beta_j-\operatorname{sign}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{jk})\beta_k
\right|,
\]
and
\[
\|\Gamma\beta\|_2^2
=
\sum_{(j,k)\in E}
|\widehat{\Sigma}_{jk}|
\left(
\beta_j-\operatorname{sign}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{jk})\beta_k
\right)^2.
\]
These expressions make explicit how the method encourages neighboring predictors in the graph to have comparable effects on the response. In particular, positively correlated predictors are encouraged to have similar coefficients, whereas negatively correlated predictors are encouraged to have coefficients with opposite signs. This approach is therefore tailored to situations in which neighboring predictors in the graph are expected to have comparable effects on the response, an assumption referred to as alignment between the covariance graph and the coefficient structure.

Under this setting, the method departs from traditional sparsity-based analyses and instead derives finite-sample mean-squared error guarantees directly in terms of graph properties, thereby providing theoretical support even when conventional high-dimensional design assumptions fail. The study further establishes optimality claims for several graph families under the stated alignment assumptions and demonstrates the practical utility of the method through both simulation studies and an application to biochemistry data. Consequently, this work represents an important development in the literature on high-dimensional regression with highly correlated predictors, as it shows that graph-based structural regularization can yield both interpretable coefficient estimates and rigorous performance guarantees in regimes where standard penalized regression methods may be unstable or theoretically difficult to justify.

\subsubsection{Bayesian Shrinkage Baselines for Correlated Predictors}

Within the Bayesian literature, shrinkage priors have provided an important baseline framework for handling high-dimensional regression problems with correlated predictors by reducing overfitting and mitigating the instability of variable selection through continuous regularization. Consider the standard Bayesian linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_n),
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta=(\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p)^\top$ is the regression coefficient vector. A particularly influential contribution is the horseshoe prior proposed by Carvalho, Polson, and Scott (2010), which represents a canonical global-local shrinkage formulation combining a sharp concentration near zero with heavy tails, thereby allowing strong shrinkage of negligible coefficients while preserving signals of substantial magnitude. Under the horseshoe prior, the coefficient-specific hierarchy is commonly written as
\[
\beta_j \mid \lambda_j,\tau,\sigma^2 \sim N(0,\sigma^2\tau^2\lambda_j^2),
\qquad j=1,\ldots,p,
\]
\[
\lambda_j \sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\tau \sim C^+(0,1),
\]
where $\lambda_j$ is a local shrinkage parameter and $\tau$ is a global shrinkage parameter, with $C^+(0,1)$ denoting the standard half-Cauchy distribution. This specification implies that most coefficients are heavily shrunk toward zero through the global-local scale $\tau\lambda_j$, while a small number of important signals may escape excessive shrinkage due to the heavy tails of the prior. Although the horseshoe prior does not explicitly impose grouping or structural dependence among predictors, it can nonetheless improve estimation stability under collinearity by adaptively shrinking regression effects according to their relative importance. This flexibility has made it an important reference point in sparse regression and signal estimation.

Building on this idea, Xu, Schmid, Makalic, Qian, and Hopper (2016) extended the horseshoe framework to grouped predictors through the grouped horseshoe prior, in which shrinkage is introduced at both the group level and the within-group level. Suppose that the predictors are partitioned into $G$ groups, and let $\beta_{gj}$ denote the coefficient for variable $j$ in group $g$. A representative grouped horseshoe specification can be written as
\[
\beta_{gj}\mid \lambda_{gj},\delta_g,\tau,\sigma^2
\sim
N(0,\sigma^2\tau^2\delta_g^2\lambda_{gj}^2),
\]
\[
\lambda_{gj}\sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\delta_g\sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\tau\sim C^+(0,1),
\]
where $\lambda_{gj}$ controls variable-specific local shrinkage within group $g$, $\delta_g$ controls group-level shrinkage, and $\tau$ is a global shrinkage parameter shared across all groups. Such a hierarchical specification is particularly useful when correlated predictors are naturally organized into meaningful blocks, as in additive models or basis expansion settings, because it allows shared regularization within groups while retaining the ability to distinguish individual effects inside each group. Accordingly, the grouped horseshoe may be viewed as a Bayesian response to correlation structures that are aligned with known predictor groupings, offering a more structured alternative to the original horseshoe prior.

Taken together, these developments illustrate that Bayesian shrinkage methods address correlated high-dimensional regression primarily through adaptive continuous shrinkage and, when appropriate structural information is available, through hierarchical grouping mechanisms that enhance interpretability and stabilize inference.

\subsubsection{Stability and Inference Under Strong Correlation}

In high-dimensional regression with strongly correlated predictors, an important methodological concern extends beyond estimation accuracy to the stability of variable selection and the validity of subsequent statistical inference. Consider the standard linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. To address the instability induced by correlation, Meinshausen and B\"uhlmann (2010) proposed stability selection, which combines subsampling with a base selection procedure, such as the lasso or elastic net, and identifies relevant variables according to their selection frequencies across resampled data sets. Let $\hat{S}^{(b)}$ denote the set of variables selected by the base procedure on the $b$th subsample, $b=1,\ldots,B$. Then the empirical selection probability for variable $j$ is defined as
\[
\hat{\Pi}_j
=
\frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^B I\bigl(j \in \hat{S}^{(b)}\bigr),
\]
and the final stable set is typically chosen as
\[
\hat{S}_{\mathrm{stable}}
=
\left\{
j:\hat{\Pi}_j \ge \pi_{\mathrm{thr}}
\right\},
\]
where $\pi_{\mathrm{thr}}$ is a pre-specified threshold. This approach was designed to improve robustness in settings where correlated predictors may lead to highly variable model selection results, and it also provides theoretical bounds on the expected number of false selections under appropriate choices of tuning parameters.

Shah and Samworth (2013) further strengthened this framework by introducing complementary pairs stability selection, which refines the resampling scheme and establishes sharper nonasymptotic error bounds without relying on strong assumptions regarding the underlying model or the base selection method. In this setting, each resampling step uses a complementary pair of subsamples, say $A_b$ and $A_b^c$, and a representative selection frequency can be written as
\[
\hat{\Pi}^{\mathrm{CPSS}}_j
=
\frac{1}{2B}
\sum_{b=1}^B
\left[
I\bigl(j \in \hat{S}(A_b)\bigr)
+
I\bigl(j \in \hat{S}(A_b^c)\bigr)
\right].
\]
Variables with large complementary-pairs selection probabilities are then retained, thereby improving the reproducibility of selected features under strong predictor dependence.

While stability selection addresses the reproducibility of selected variables, another major direction has focused on valid inference after regularized estimation. In this regard, van de Geer, B\"uhlmann, Ritov, and Dezeure (2014) developed debiased or desparsified estimators for low-dimensional parameters in high-dimensional models, thereby enabling asymptotically normal inference, confidence intervals, and hypothesis tests even under correlated Gaussian, sub-Gaussian, or bounded designs. Starting from a regularized estimator such as the lasso, $\hat{\beta}^{\mathrm{lasso}}$, a representative debiased estimator for coefficient $j$ takes the form
\[
\tilde{\beta}_j
=
\hat{\beta}^{\mathrm{lasso}}_j
+
\frac{1}{n}\hat{\theta}_j^{\top}X^{\top}\bigl(y-X\hat{\beta}^{\mathrm{lasso}}\bigr),
\]
where $\hat{\theta}_j$ is an estimated score or precision-direction vector used to remove the first-order bias introduced by penalization. Under suitable regularity conditions, this corrected estimator satisfies an asymptotic normality result of the form
\[
\frac{\sqrt{n}\,(\tilde{\beta}_j-\beta_j)}{\hat{\sigma}_j}
\;\overset{d}{\longrightarrow}\;
N(0,1),
\]
which provides the basis for confidence intervals and hypothesis tests. Their work provides an important theoretical foundation for post-selection inference in both linear models and generalized linear models with convex loss functions.

A related development that explicitly exploits feature dependence is the knockoff framework of Barber and Cand\`es (2015), which constructs synthetic variables that replicate the dependence structure of the original predictors and then compares each original variable with its knockoff counterpart. In the fixed-design setting, the knockoff matrix $\tilde{X}$ is constructed so that
\[
\tilde{X}^{\top}\tilde{X}=X^{\top}X,
\qquad
X^{\top}\tilde{X}=X^{\top}X-\operatorname{diag}(s),
\]
for a suitable nonnegative vector $s$, thereby ensuring that the knockoff variables mimic the correlation structure of the original predictors. After fitting a lasso-type procedure to the augmented design $[X\ \tilde{X}]$, one may define feature statistics such as
\[
W_j
=
|\hat{\beta}_j|-|\hat{\beta}_{j+p}|,
\]
where $\hat{\beta}_j$ and $\hat{\beta}_{j+p}$ denote the fitted coefficients for the original variable and its knockoff counterpart, respectively. Variables are then selected according to
\[
\hat{S}_{\mathrm{KO}}
=
\{j: W_j \ge T\},
\]
where $T$ is a data-dependent threshold chosen to control the false discovery rate. This construction allows variable selection procedures based on lasso-type statistics to achieve finite-sample false discovery rate control while accounting directly for correlation among predictors.

Taken together, these contributions demonstrate that, under strong predictor dependence, modern high-dimensional regression methodology must address not only shrinkage and selection but also the reproducibility of selected features and the validity of inferential conclusions, and that resampling-based stability methods, debiasing techniques, and knockoff constructions provide three influential and complementary solutions to these challenges.

\subsection{Graph Laplacian Used in Regression}
Graph Laplacian regression intorduces a predictor graph and uses the Laplacian to encourage coefficient smoothness on that graph. Frequentist approaches typically combine $\ell_1$ sparsity with a Laplacian quadratic form $\beta^\top L \beta$; Bayesian approaches encode $L$ as a prior precision (or build thresholded variants to induce selection).

Given a weighted graph over predictors, the Laplacian $L$ yields $\beta^\top L \beta = \Sum_{(u,v)\in E} w_{uv}(\beta_u-\beta_v)^2$ (up to normalization), penalizing differences across connected predictors. If edges connect highly correlated variables or scientifically linked features, Laplacian smoothing preduces a structured grouping effect aligned with correlation. A central risk is oversmoothing bias (especially when neighboring effects differ in sign), motivating sign-adaptive constructions and combinations with nonconvex sparsity.


\subsubsection{Frequentist Laplacian penalties: network-constrained regression and GRACE}

Li and Li (2008) introduced one of the earliest regression frameworks to incorporate graph-structured prior information through a Laplacian-based penalty, proposing a network-constrained regularization method that combines sparsity with smoothness over a known biological network. Consider the standard linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. Let the underlying graph be denoted by $G=(V,E,W)$, where $V$ is the set of predictors, $E$ is the set of edges, and $w(u,v)$ is the weight for edge $(u,v)$. If $d_u=\sum_{v:u\sim v} w(u,v)$ denotes the degree of node $u$, then the normalized graph Laplacian can be written as
\[
L(u,v)=
\begin{cases}
1-\dfrac{w(u,v)}{d_u}, & u=v,\ d_u\neq 0,\\[6pt]
-\dfrac{w(u,v)}{\sqrt{d_ud_v}}, & u\sim v,\\[6pt]
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
In this approach, the regression coefficients are estimated under a penalty structure that not only encourages variable selection but also promotes similarity among coefficients corresponding to neighboring genes or predictors on the network. Specifically, Li and Li (2008) considered the criterion
\[
\hat{\beta}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_1 \|\beta\|_1
+
\lambda_2 \beta^\top L \beta
\right\},
\]
where the $\ell_1$ term encourages sparsity and the quadratic Laplacian term induces smoothness over the network. Because
\[
\beta^\top L\beta
=
\sum_{u\sim v}
w(u,v)
\left(
\frac{\beta_u}{\sqrt{d_u}}
-
\frac{\beta_v}{\sqrt{d_v}}
\right)^2,
\]
the penalty directly shrinks the scaled coefficients of adjacent nodes toward one another. This formulation is particularly useful when neighboring predictors are expected to have similar effects, since it allows the regression model to reflect underlying biological pathway structure and improves interpretability in high-dimensional genomic applications. This work demonstrated that network information can be directly embedded into the estimation procedure, making it possible to identify not only individual relevant predictors but also functionally related subnetworks.

Building on this idea, Li and Li (2010) further developed the GRACE framework for regression with graph-structured covariates, in which an $\ell_1$ penalty is combined with a Laplacian quadratic penalty to achieve simultaneous sparsity and graph-aware smoothing. A representative GRACE estimator may be written as
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\mathrm{GRACE}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2n}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\lambda_1 \|\beta\|_1
+
\lambda_2 \beta^\top L \beta
\right\},
\]
where $L$ is the graph Laplacian matrix associated with the known predictor network. In contrast to conventional sparsity-based methods that treat predictors independently, GRACE explicitly exploits known relationships among predictors so that coefficients associated with adjacent nodes in the graph are encouraged to be similar, thereby stabilizing estimation when the graph structure aligns with the correlation pattern among covariates. To address situations in which neighboring predictors may have regression effects of opposite signs, the authors also proposed the adaptive variant aGRACE, which modifies the graph-smoothing term using sign information from an initial estimator and thereby mitigates the oversmoothing problem that may arise under standard Laplacian regularization.

In addition to methodological development, this study established estimation and model selection consistency under suitable regularity conditions, thereby providing a stronger theoretical foundation for graph-constrained regression in both fixed and diverging dimensional settings. Taken together, these studies constitute a foundational line of research in frequentist Laplacian regularization, showing that graph-based penalties can effectively integrate structural information into regression analysis and improve variable selection and coefficient estimation in applications involving networked or biologically connected predictors.


\subsubsection{Nonconvex Sparsity + Laplacian Smoothing: SLS}

Huang, Ma, Li, and Zhang (2011) proposed the sparse Laplacian shrinkage (SLS) estimator as a regression framework that integrates sparsity induction with graph-based coefficient smoothing for high-dimensional settings. Consider the standard linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. Specifically, the method combines the minimax concave penalty (MCP), which is designed to reduce the estimation bias commonly associated with convex penalties while promoting sparse variable selection, with a Laplacian quadratic penalty that encourages similar regression coefficients for predictors connected through a graph representing correlation patterns or structural relationships among covariates. The SLS estimator can be written as
\[
\hat{\beta}^{\mathrm{SLS}}
=
\arg\min_{\beta}
\left\{
\frac{1}{2n}\|y-X\beta\|_2^2
+
\sum_{j=1}^p \rho\bigl(|\beta_j|;\lambda_1,\gamma\bigr)
+
\frac{\lambda_2}{2}\beta^\top L\beta
\right\},
\]
where $\rho(\cdot;\lambda_1,\gamma)$ denotes the MCP, $\lambda_1>0$ controls sparsity, $\lambda_2>0$ controls graph smoothing, and $L$ is the graph Laplacian matrix associated with the predictor network. A common form of the MCP is
\[
\rho(t;\lambda_1,\gamma)
=
\lambda_1 \int_0^t \left(1-\frac{x}{\gamma\lambda_1}\right)_+ dx,
\qquad t\ge 0,
\]
where $\gamma>1$ is a shape parameter and $(a)_+ = \max(a,0)$. Equivalently, its derivative is
\[
\rho'(t;\lambda_1,\gamma)
=
\lambda_1\left(1-\frac{t}{\gamma\lambda_1}\right)_+,
\qquad t\ge 0,
\]
which shows that the penalty behaves like an $\ell_1$ penalty near zero but applies less shrinkage to large coefficients, thereby reducing estimation bias relative to convex penalties.

The Laplacian quadratic term can be expressed as
\[
\beta^\top L\beta
=
\sum_{j<k} a_{jk}(\beta_j-\beta_k)^2,
\]
or, more generally, in weighted form according to the graph structure. Thus, the Laplacian component encourages coefficients of connected predictors to be similar, stabilizing estimation by borrowing structural information across related variables. Through this construction, SLS is able to address correlated predictors in a more flexible manner than purely sparsity-based procedures, since the Laplacian component stabilizes estimation by borrowing structural information across related variables, whereas the MCP component improves support recovery by alleviating excessive shrinkage of large coefficients.

From a theoretical perspective, the study established a generalized grouping property and showed that, under suitable regularity conditions in sparse high-dimensional regimes with $p \gg n$, the SLS estimator enjoys the oracle property, in the sense that it achieves selection consistency and is asymptotically equivalent, with high probability, to an oracle Laplacian shrinkage estimator constructed with knowledge of the true support. Consequently, this work represents an important development in the literature on graph-regularized high-dimensional regression, as it demonstrates that the combination of nonconvex sparsity and Laplacian smoothing can simultaneously improve variable selection accuracy, reduce bias, and exploit correlation structure among predictors.




\subsubsection{Bayesian Laplacian Priors and Thresholded Variants}

Within the Bayesian literature, graph Laplacian priors have provided a principled framework for incorporating structural dependence among predictors into high-dimensional regression models. Consider the standard Bayesian regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_n),
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. Liu, Chakraborty, Li, Liu, and Lozano (2014) proposed Bayesian regularization via graph Laplacian, in which dependence among regression coefficients is modeled directly through a graph Laplacian matrix in the prior precision structure. A representative form of such a prior can be written as
\[
\beta \mid \sigma^2,\tau^2,L
\sim
N\!\left(0,\sigma^2\tau^2 (L+\xi I_p)^{-1}\right),
\]
where $L$ is a graph Laplacian matrix associated with the predictor network and $\xi>0$ is a small regularization constant ensuring positive definiteness. Equivalently, the prior density may be expressed as
\[
\pi(\beta \mid \sigma^2,\tau^2,L)
\propto
\exp\!\left\{
-\frac{1}{2\sigma^2\tau^2}\beta^\top (L+\xi I_p)\beta
\right\}.
\]
This formulation induces structured shrinkage by allowing prior dependence among coefficients associated with related predictors, thereby extending conventional Bayesian shrinkage methods to settings where network or graph information is available. Since
\[
\beta^\top L \beta
=
\sum_{(j,k)\in E} w_{jk}(\beta_j-\beta_k)^2,
\]
the graph Laplacian component encourages neighboring coefficients to be similar, while the diagonal component provides overall shrinkage and numerical stability.

Building on this perspective, Chakraborty and Lozano (2019) developed a graph Laplacian prior for Bayesian variable selection and grouping, with the specific aim of simultaneously performing coefficient shrinkage, variable selection, and the identification of predictive clusters. In this setting, a representative prior specification may be written hierarchically as
\[
\beta \mid \Lambda,\sigma^2 \sim N(0,\sigma^2\Lambda^{-1}),
\]
where the precision matrix $\Lambda$ is constructed so that its off-diagonal entries encode graph-based relationships and its diagonal entries control shrinkage and positive definiteness. Such a formulation allows coefficients corresponding to connected predictors to borrow strength from one another while also supporting sparse estimation and cluster formation. By coupling coefficients through a Laplacian-based prior, the method is particularly suited to problems in which correlated predictors are expected to have similar regression effects, thereby promoting coefficient similarity while retaining a Bayesian mechanism for sparse model selection.

A further refinement was introduced by Cai, Kang, and Yu (2020), who proposed the thresholded graph Laplacian Gaussian prior for network marker selection in generalized linear models. In this approach, the graph Laplacian component captures the global dependence structure among neighboring markers, whereas an additional thresholding mechanism induces sparsity and reconciles the competing goals of smoothing and variable selection. Specifically, letting $L$ denote the normalized graph Laplacian matrix, they consider the decomposition
\[
\beta = \alpha \circ t_{\lambda}(\gamma),
\]
where $\circ$ denotes elementwise multiplication and
\[
t_{\lambda}(\gamma)
=
\bigl(I(|\gamma_1|>\lambda),\ldots,I(|\gamma_p|>\lambda)\bigr)^\top.
\]
The latent vectors are assigned Gaussian priors of the form
\[
\gamma \sim N\!\left(0,\sigma_\gamma^2 (L+\varepsilon I_p)^{-1}\right),
\qquad
\alpha \sim N(0,\sigma_\alpha^2 I_p),
\]
with $\varepsilon>0$ included to ensure invertibility. Under this formulation, $\gamma$ controls network-structured selection through the thresholding rule, while $\alpha$ controls effect sizes for the selected predictors. Thus, the graph Laplacian induces dependence across neighboring markers, whereas the thresholding operator yields explicit sparsity in $\beta$. Their method was accompanied by posterior consistency results under regimes with diverging network size, thereby providing a stronger theoretical justification for Bayesian graph-based regularization in high-dimensional settings.

Taken together, these studies demonstrate that Bayesian Laplacian priors offer a flexible and coherent approach to regression with network-structured or highly correlated predictors by embedding graph dependence directly into the prior distribution and, in more recent developments, combining such dependence modeling with explicit mechanisms for sparse selection and grouping.

\subsection{TPBN/TPB Shrinkage Priors and Structured Bayesian Regression Under Correlation}
The acronym \textbf{TPBN} is ambiguous in general usage. Follow the \textbf{primary definition in Armagan et al.\ (2011)}: the \textbf{TPB normal scale mixture} yields a marginal distribution denoted \textbf{TPBN$(a,b,\phi)$} (Three-Parameter Beta Normal), which provides a large family of global--local shrinkage priors.


\subsubsection{TPBN Definition and Unification via TPB Mixing}

Armagan, Dunson, and Clyde (2011) introduced the TPBN prior within the broader framework of generalized beta mixtures of Gaussians, providing a unified Bayesian shrinkage construction for high-dimensional regression. Consider the standard Bayesian linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_n),
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta=(\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p)^\top$ is the regression coefficient vector. Their formulation is based on assigning a three-parameter beta (TPB) distribution to a latent shrinkage coefficient $\rho_j$, whose density is given by
\[
f(\rho_j;a,b,\phi)
=
\frac{\Gamma(a+b)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}
\phi^{\,b}
\rho_j^{\,b-1}
(1-\rho_j)^{a-1}
\{1+(\phi-1)\rho_j\}^{-(a+b)},
\qquad 0<\rho_j<1,
\]
where $a>0$, $b>0$, and $\phi>0$. This distribution is denoted by
\[
\rho_j \sim \mathrm{TPB}(a,b,\phi).
\]
The TPBN prior is then obtained by mixing a normal distribution over its scale through $\rho_j$, namely
\[
\beta_j \mid \rho_j,\sigma^2
\sim
N\!\left(0,\sigma^2\left(\rho_j^{-1}-1\right)\right),
\qquad
\rho_j \sim \mathrm{TPB}(a,b,\phi),
\]
and the resulting marginal prior on $\beta_j$ is referred to as
\[
\beta_j \sim \mathrm{TPBN}(a,b,\phi).
\]
This representation is important because it places a wide class of continuous shrinkage priors under a common hierarchical framework, thereby clarifying the relationships among existing Bayesian regularization methods and offering a flexible basis for further methodological development. In particular, smaller values of $a$ lead to a prior that is more concentrated near zero, whereas smaller values of $b$ produce heavier tails; the global parameter $\phi$ controls the overall degree of shrinkage.

An important feature of this construction is that it admits equivalent hierarchical representations. In particular, if
\[
\beta_j \sim \mathrm{TPBN}(a,b,\phi),
\]
then an equivalent hierarchy is
\[
\beta_j \mid \tau_j,\sigma^2 \sim N(0,\sigma^2\tau_j),
\qquad
\tau_j \mid \lambda_j \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(a,\lambda_j),
\qquad
\lambda_j \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(b,\phi),
\]
where $\phi$ acts as a global shrinkage parameter. Equivalently, the local variance component satisfies
\[
\frac{\tau_j}{\phi} \sim \beta'(a,b),
\]
where $\beta'(a,b)$ denotes the inverted-beta distribution. This equivalence is central to the TPBN framework because it unifies several previously proposed shrinkage priors through a common mixing mechanism and also leads to convenient conjugate computations in linear models.

In its original form, the TPBN prior is specified componentwise and therefore does not directly encode dependence among predictors; however, it provides a fundamental shrinkage backbone upon which later correlation-aware extensions, such as grouped or graph-structured priors, can be constructed. In addition to its distributional unification, the study also emphasized the computational advantages of the TPBN framework, including scalable approximate inference strategies such as variational Bayes, which make it particularly suitable for massive regression problems. Consequently, this work occupies a central position in the literature on Bayesian shrinkage priors, both as a formal definition of the TPBN family and as a general framework for understanding and extending continuous shrinkage methods in high-dimensional regression.


\subsubsection{Canonical Global-Local Priors (Horseshoe and Variants)}

Within the landscape of Bayesian global-local shrinkage priors, the horseshoe prior has emerged as one of the most influential formulations and may be regarded as a canonical representative of the broader TPBN-like family of continuous shrinkage models. Consider the standard Bayesian linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_n),
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta=(\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p)^\top$ is the regression coefficient vector. As introduced by Carvalho, Polson, and Scott (2010), the horseshoe prior combines a global shrinkage parameter with local coefficient-specific scales, thereby allowing negligible effects to be strongly shrunk toward zero while preserving substantial signals through its heavy-tailed behavior. A standard hierarchical representation is
\[
\beta_j \mid \lambda_j,\tau,\sigma^2 \sim N(0,\sigma^2\tau^2\lambda_j^2),
\qquad j=1,\ldots,p,
\]
\[
\lambda_j \sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\tau \sim C^+(0,1),
\]
where $\lambda_j$ is a local shrinkage parameter and $\tau$ is a global shrinkage parameter. This property has made the horseshoe prior particularly attractive for sparse high-dimensional regression and signal estimation.

An equivalent and often useful way to understand the amount of shrinkage is through the shrinkage factor
\[
\kappa_j
=
\frac{1}{1+n\sigma^{-2}\tau^2\lambda_j^2},
\]
so that $1-\kappa_j$ can be interpreted as the extent to which coefficient $\beta_j$ escapes complete shrinkage toward zero. Small signals tend to have $\kappa_j$ close to $1$, whereas important signals tend to have $\kappa_j$ closer to $0$.

Building on this framework, Bhadra, Datta, Polson, and Willard (2017) proposed the horseshoe+ prior, which introduces an additional hierarchical shrinkage layer in order to improve the separation between signal and noise in ultra-sparse settings. A representative hierarchical form is
\[
\beta_j \mid \lambda_j,\eta_j,\tau,\sigma^2
\sim
N(0,\sigma^2\tau^2\lambda_j^2\eta_j^2),
\]
\[
\lambda_j \sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\eta_j \sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\tau \sim C^+(0,1).
\]
Thus, relative to the original horseshoe prior, horseshoe+ adds an extra local half-Cauchy mixing variable $\eta_j$, which yields a sharper spike near zero and heavier tails. Although neither the horseshoe nor the horseshoe+ prior explicitly encodes correlation structure among predictors in their baseline forms, both methods can nevertheless enhance stability under correlated designs through adaptive continuous shrinkage, and they have served as important foundations for later structured extensions.

An additional practical issue within this class of priors concerns the specification of the global shrinkage parameter $\tau$, whose prior distribution can substantially influence posterior regularization behavior. In this regard, Piironen and Vehtari (2017) provided a systematic analysis of hyperprior choice for $\tau$, establishing an interpretable relationship between the global shrinkage level and the effective number of nonzero coefficients in the model. Specifically, they define the effective number of nonzero coefficients as
\[
m_{\mathrm{eff}}
=
\sum_{j=1}^D (1-\kappa_j),
\]
and show that
\[
E(m_{\mathrm{eff}}\mid \tau,\sigma)
=
\frac{\sigma^{-1}\tau\sqrt{n}}{1+\sigma^{-1}\tau\sqrt{n}}\,D.
\]
If $p_0$ denotes a prior guess for the number of relevant predictors, this leads to the reference value
\[
\tau_0
=
\frac{p_0}{D-p_0}\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}},
\]
which provides a principled way to calibrate the prior on $\tau$ according to prior beliefs about sparsity. Their work offered principled guidance for selecting hyperpriors based on prior beliefs about sparsity, thereby improving the robustness and practical calibration of horseshoe-based inference.

Taken together, these studies illustrate that horseshoe-type priors constitute a central component of the modern Bayesian shrinkage literature, not only because of their theoretical and empirical effectiveness in sparse regression, but also because of their role in motivating subsequent extensions for more complex high-dimensional settings.


\subsubsection{Theory: Posterior Consistency and Uncertainty Quantification}

From a theoretical perspective, an important strand of the TPBN and broader Bayesian shrinkage literature has focused on establishing the validity of posterior inference in high-dimensional settings. Consider the standard linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta_0 + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_n),
\]
where $\beta_0 \in \mathbb{R}^p$ denotes the true regression coefficient vector. Armagan, Dunson, Lee, Bajwa, and Strawn (2013) investigated posterior consistency in linear regression models under shrinkage priors when the number of predictors grows with the sample size, providing sufficient conditions under which the posterior distribution concentrates around the true regression coefficients under sparsity assumptions. A representative expression of posterior consistency is
\[
\Pi_n\!\left(
\beta:\|\beta-\beta_0\|_2>\varepsilon
\,\middle|\,
y
\right)
\longrightarrow 0
\qquad \text{as } n\to\infty,
\]
for every $\varepsilon>0$, where $\Pi_n(\cdot\mid y)$ denotes the posterior distribution. Their results offered a fundamental theoretical justification for the use of continuous shrinkage priors in large-$p$ regression, demonstrating that such priors can retain desirable asymptotic behavior beyond conventional low-dimensional settings, with the role of predictor correlation entering through the design conditions required for posterior concentration.

A related but distinct theoretical concern is uncertainty quantification under global-local shrinkage priors. In this regard, van der Pas, Szab\'o, and van der Vaart (2017) studied credible sets and marginal credible intervals for the horseshoe prior in sparse normal means models, with particular attention to the frequentist coverage properties of Bayesian uncertainty statements. In the sparse normal means setting,
\[
Y_i=\theta_i+\varepsilon_i,
\qquad
\varepsilon_i \sim N(0,1),
\qquad i=1,\ldots,n,
\]
and the horseshoe prior is imposed on the coordinates $\theta_i$. A $(1-\alpha)$ posterior credible ball centered at a posterior location estimator $\hat{\theta}$ can be written as
\[
C_\alpha(Y)
=
\left\{
\theta:\|\theta-\hat{\theta}\|_2 \le r_\alpha(Y)
\right\},
\]
where the radius $r_\alpha(Y)$ is chosen so that
\[
\Pi\!\left(
\theta:\|\theta-\hat{\theta}\|_2 \le r_\alpha(Y)
\,\middle|\,
Y
\right)
=
1-\alpha.
\]
Similarly, a marginal $(1-\alpha)$ credible interval for coordinate $\theta_i$ may be written in posterior-quantile form as
\[
I_i(Y)
=
\bigl[
q_{i,\alpha/2}(Y),\, q_{i,1-\alpha/2}(Y)
\bigr],
\]
where $q_{i,\alpha/2}(Y)$ and $q_{i,1-\alpha/2}(Y)$ denote the corresponding posterior quantiles of $\theta_i$. Their analysis clarified the circumstances under which horseshoe-based credible regions can achieve appropriate coverage and near-optimal size, while also showing that adaptive credible sets cannot be uniformly honest over the full parameter space. These findings are important because they reveal both the strengths and limitations of Bayesian shrinkage priors for uncertainty quantification, especially in sparse high-dimensional problems where shrinkage may induce overconfidence in certain regimes.

Taken together, these studies provide an essential theoretical foundation for the TPBN-related literature by showing that continuous shrinkage priors are theoretically meaningful both in terms of posterior concentration and in terms of the behavior of Bayesian uncertainty quantification.

\subsubsection{Structured Extensions for Correlated Predictors: Groups and Graphs}

Within the development of structured Bayesian shrinkage for correlated predictors, several extensions have expanded the global-local prior framework beyond componentwise regularization toward formulations that can better accommodate group and graph dependence. Consider the standard Bayesian linear regression model
\[
y = X\beta + \varepsilon,
\qquad
\varepsilon \sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_n),
\]
where $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the response vector, $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ is the design matrix, and $\beta \in \mathbb{R}^p$ is the regression coefficient vector. One important example is the grouped horseshoe prior of Xu et al.\ (2016), which extends the original horseshoe by introducing hierarchical shrinkage at both the group level and the within-group level, thereby allowing predictors that belong to the same structural block to share a common degree of regularization while preserving local adaptivity for individual coefficients. If predictor $j$ belongs to group $g$, a representative grouped horseshoe hierarchy can be written as
\[
\beta_{gj}\mid \lambda_{gj},\delta_g,\tau,\sigma^2
\sim
N(0,\sigma^2\tau^2\delta_g^2\lambda_{gj}^2),
\]
\[
\lambda_{gj}\sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\delta_g\sim C^+(0,1),
\qquad
\tau\sim C^+(0,1),
\]
where $\lambda_{gj}$ is a local shrinkage parameter, $\delta_g$ is a group-level shrinkage parameter, and $\tau$ is a global shrinkage parameter. This type of extension is particularly relevant when predictor correlation is aligned with known groupings, such as additive components or basis expansions.

In a related direction, Armagan, Dunson, and Lee (2013) proposed the generalized double Pareto (GDP) shrinkage prior, which provides a heavy-tailed alternative to other global-local priors by bringing Laplace-like concentration near zero and Student-$t$-like tail behavior. The GDP density for a coefficient $\beta_j$ may be written as
\[
\pi(\beta_j\mid \xi,\alpha)
=
\frac{1}{2\xi}
\left(
1+\frac{|\beta_j|}{\alpha \xi}
\right)^{-(\alpha+1)},
\qquad \xi>0,\ \alpha>0,
\]
and it admits the scale-mixture representation
\[
\beta_j\mid \tau_j,\sigma^2 \sim N(0,\sigma^2\tau_j),
\qquad
\tau_j\mid \lambda_j \sim \mathrm{Exp}\!\left(\frac{\lambda_j^2}{2}\right),
\qquad
\lambda_j \sim \mathrm{Ga}(\alpha,\eta),
\]
with $\xi=\eta/\alpha$. Although the GDP prior is typically specified componentwise, it is important in the broader literature because it offers sparse estimation with reduced bias for large coefficients and thus complements TPBN and horseshoe-type priors as a flexible shrinkage family for high-dimensional regression.

Polson and Scott (2012) further contributed to this line of work by developing a L\'evy-process perspective on local shrinkage rules, thereby clarifying the conceptual relationship between Bayesian prior constructions and frequentist penalty functions in regularized regression. In a generic local-scale mixture formulation,
\[
\beta_j \mid \tau_j \sim N(0,\tau_j),
\qquad
\tau_j \sim p(\tau_j),
\]
which induces the marginal prior
\[
p(\beta_j)=\int_0^\infty N(\beta_j\mid 0,\tau_j)\,p(\tau_j)\,d\tau_j.
\]
The corresponding regularization penalty can then be expressed as
\[
m(\beta_j)=-\log p(\beta_j),
\]
showing how a shrinkage prior can be interpreted as a penalty function in penalized regression. Although explicit correlation modeling is not built into the baseline formulation, this perspective offers a general theoretical basis upon which structured extensions involving groups or graphs can be layered.

More directly related to graph-structured dependence are Bayesian Laplacian-type priors such as BRGL and TGLG, which illustrate how structured dependence and sparsity may be combined within a unified Bayesian framework. In these models, the graph Laplacian is used in the prior precision structure to encode dependence among neighboring or correlated predictors. A representative BRGL-type prior may be written as
\[
\beta \mid \sigma^2,\tau^2,L
\sim
N\!\left(0,\sigma^2\tau^2(L+\xi I_p)^{-1}\right),
\]
where $L$ is a graph Laplacian matrix and $\xi>0$ ensures positive definiteness. Since
\[
\beta^\top L\beta
=
\sum_{(j,k)\in E} w_{jk}(\beta_j-\beta_k)^2,
\]
this prior encourages neighboring coefficients to be similar.

A further refinement appears in the thresholded graph Laplacian Gaussian prior (TGLG), where sparsity is induced through an additional thresholding mechanism. A representative specification is
\[
\beta = \alpha \circ t_{\lambda}(\gamma),
\]
where $\circ$ denotes elementwise multiplication and
\[
t_{\lambda}(\gamma)
=
\bigl(I(|\gamma_1|>\lambda),\ldots,I(|\gamma_p|>\lambda)\bigr)^\top.
\]
The latent variables are assigned priors
\[
\gamma \sim N\!\left(0,\sigma_\gamma^2(L+\varepsilon I_p)^{-1}\right),
\qquad
\alpha \sim N(0,\sigma_\alpha^2 I_p),
\]
so that the graph Laplacian captures dependence among neighboring predictors while the thresholding operator yields explicit sparsity in $\beta$. These developments are particularly important because they show how TPBN-like shrinkage ideas can be extended beyond independent coefficient modeling to accommodate structural information among predictors, even when such methods are not explicitly formulated under the TPBN label.

Collectively, this strand of research demonstrates that modern Bayesian shrinkage methodology has evolved from purely componentwise priors to increasingly structured formulations capable of integrating group information, graph dependence, and sparsity within a coherent hierarchical modeling framework.

\subsection{Application and Foundation of LKJ/Onion Priors in Bayesian}

$LKJ$ priors (from the Lewandowski-Kurowicka-Joe onion method) are now a standard flexible prior on correlation matrices in Bayesian regression. Let
\[
\Sigma = D R D,
\qquad
D=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_K),
\]
where $R$ is a $K\times K$ correlation matrix and $D$ contains marginal scale parameters. This decomposition is important because it separates marginal variances from dependence structure. Lewandowski et al.\ (2009) introduced the onion/vine method for generating random correlation matrices, yielding the $LKJ$ prior, whose density for a correlation matrix $R$ is
\[
\pi(R\mid \eta)\propto \det(R)^{\eta-1},
\qquad \eta>0.
\]
Stan's documentation explicitly recommends decomposing a covariance into scales and a correlation matrix and giving the latter an $LKJ(\eta)$ prior. In a hierarchical Bayesian regression setting, a typical prior specification may therefore be written as
\[
\beta_j \sim N(\mu,\Sigma),
\qquad
\Sigma = D R D,
\qquad
R \sim LKJ(\eta),
\]
or, equivalently, in Cholesky form,
\[
R = L_R L_R^\top,
\qquad
L_R \sim LKJ\text{-Cholesky}(\eta).
\]
In particular, Stan's user guide notes that choosing $\eta=1$ yields a uniform prior over correlation matrices, while $\eta>1$ concentrates density near the identity matrix and therefore favors weaker correlations. Thus, $LKJ$ has become a default weakly informative prior for correlation matrices in hierarchical modeling. Unlike the inverse-Wishart prior, $LKJ$ cleanly separates scales from correlation, which makes prior specification more interpretable and more flexible in regression models with structured covariance assumptions. Stan's functions reference also shows that
\[
LKJ(R\mid \eta)\propto \det(R)^{\eta-1},
\]
underscoring its role in Stan-based Bayesian regression.

\subsubsection{Applications in Hierarchical and Mixed-Effects Models}
In hierarchical or mixed models, $LKJ$ priors typically regularize the random-effect correlation matrix. For example, Brilleman et al.\ (2017) fit a Bayesian piecewise-linear mixed model (via Stan) by decomposing the random-effects covariance into standard deviations and a correlation matrix, then placing Stan's $LKJ_{corr}(\eta)$ prior on that correlation. They set $\eta$ implicitly to $1$, which is the default uniform prior, used half-Cauchy priors on the scales, and ran HMC in Stan. This approach matches Stan's recommended strategy. In general, analysts specify $\Omega = $Corr(random effects) with $\Omega \sim LKJ(\eta)$, $\eta$ often unspecified or set $\ge 1$ for weak informativeness. The result is a well-behaved posterior for group-level effects with interpretable shrinkage: higher $\eta$ yields less spurious correlation, and $\eta = 1$ yields no prior preference.

\subsubsection{Applications in Covariance Regression Models}
$LKJ$ priors are also used in covariance-regression settings. Seiler and Holmes (2017) modeled functional connectivity difference via a covariance regression; they parameterized each covariance term with its own correlation matrix and assigned each an $LKJ(\eta)$ prior. In practice they use $\eta = 1$ for each term and half-Cauchy on scales. Their model was implemented in Stan, via the CovRegFC R package, with Stan code made public. Similarly, Park (2024) proposed a "Covariate-assisted principal regression" for brain connectivity
, decomposing covariance as $diag(\omega)\Omegadiag(\omega)$ and using $\omega\sim half-Cauchy, \Omega \sim LKJ(\eta)$. He allows $\eta > 0$ (shape parameter unspecified) and performs HMC via Stan. These examples show that $LKJ$ priors can flexibly regularize correlation structures in regression on covariance matrices.

\subsubsection{Applications in Joint Models}
In Bayesian joint models, $LKJ$ priors often regularize the random-effects correlation. Mauff et al.\ (2020) fitted a multivariate joint model with several biomarkers: they decompose the random-effects covariance and put an $LKJ(\zeta)$ prior on the correlation with $\zeta = 1.5$ and half-$t$ priors on the scales. Estimation was via a two-stage MCMC algorithm. Yu et al.\ (2024) built a joint model with threshold regression for biomarkers and menopause timing. They used Stan (rstan) with HMC: random-effects SDs had half-Cauchy priors, and the correlation matrix had an $LKJ(\eta)$ prior with $\eta = 1$. Finally, Palma et al.\ (2025) presented a Bayesian location-scale joint model. They similarly used half-$t$ priors on SDs and $LKJ(\eta)$ on the random-effects correlation. In all these joint-model examples, the $LKJ$ prior was placed on the correlation matrix $(\Omega)$ of random effects, providing interpretable shrinkage; typically $\eta$ is set to $1$ or a mild value.

\subsubsection{Discussion}
$LKJ$ priors are favored for their interpretability, but users note their behavor depends on dimension and $\eta$. Stan's guide observes that for high-dimensional $\Omega$, even a flat $LKJ(\eta = 1)$ yields marginal correlations concentrated near zero, so one may choose $\eta > 1$ for stronger shrinkage. Unlike inverse-Wishart, LKJ separates scale and correlation priors, which simplifies hyperparameter choice. However, some have reported that flat $LKJ$ priors can under-estimate true correlations in large models. Alternatives include hierarchical or factor priors on $\Omega$, but these are less common in applied regression. In practice, $LKJ(onion)$ priors serve as a default weakly-informative prior for correlations.

