The Phase 2 simulation result shows at Table 3 and Table 4. For the independent case, $r = 0$, TPBN-MBSP performs better than proposed method in estimating the truly nonzero coefficients $X_1\sim X_7$. The estimates from TPBN-MBSP are much closer to the true values, especially for $X_1\sim X_5$, whereas proposed method shows stronger shrinkage toward zero. This result is reasonable because when the predictors are independent, there is no correlation structure for proposed method to exploit. Since proposed method is designed to incorporate dependence information among regression coefficients through the modified covariance structure, this additional structure may become unnecessary or even slightly over-regularizing in the independent setting. For the moderate correlation case, $r=0.5$, TPBN-MBSP still gives more accurate estimates for the nonzero coefficients $X_1\sim X_7$. In particular, the estimates of $X_1, X_2, X_6$ and $X_7$ are very close to their true values under TPBN-MBSP. However, proposed method begins to show an advantage for the correlated null variables $X_8 \sim X_{14}$. These variables have true coefficients equal to zero but are generated with correlation structures. Compared with TPBN-MBSP, proposed method shrinks these correlated noise variables more strongly toward zero and produces smaller posterior standard deviations for this block. This suggests that when moderate correlation is present, proposed method is more conservative in preventing irrelevant but correlated predictors from begin falsely identified as important. For the high correlation case, $r=0.9$, the difference between the two methods becomes more apparent. TPBN-MBSP still performs better in estimating some individual nonzero coefficients. However, for the correlated group $X_3, X4, X_5$, whose true coefficients are $(-1, -1, 2)$, both methods are affected by the strong dependence among predictors. In this setting, proposed method shows its advantage mainly in controlling the estimates of the null correlated variables $X_8 \sim X_{14}$. The estimates from proposed method are close to zero and have smaller variation than those from TPBN-MBSP. This indicates that proposed method is more robust when highly correlated irrelevant predictors exist, even though it may introduce stronger shrinkage for some truly nonzero coefficients. For the extremely high correlation case, $r = 0.999$, proposed method has its clearest advantage. Under TPBN-MBSP, the estimates of the null variables $X_8 \sim X_{11}$ become unstable, and their posterior standard deviations are very large. For example, some zero coefficients are estimated far away from zero, which indicates that TPBN-MBSP is strongly affected by near-perfect multicollinearity. In contrast, proposed method successfully shrinks $X_8 \sim X_{14}$ close to zero and keeps their posterior standard deviations much smaller. Although both methods have difficulty recovering the individual coefficients of the highly correlated active group $X_3, X_4, X_5$, proposed method provides much more stable estimates for the correlated noise variables. Therefore, under extremely, strong correlation, proposed method is more reliable in reducing false signals and controlling posterior uncertainty. Overall, TPBN-MBSP performs better when the predictors are independent or only moderately correlated, especially for recovering the individual nonzero coefficients $X_1 \sim X_7$. However, as the correlation among predictors increases, proposed method becomes more advantageous. Its main strength is not necessarily improving the estimation of every individual nonzero, but rather stabilizing the estimation under correlation predictor structures. The results suggest that the proposed method is especially useful for high-dimensional regression with strong predictor correlation, where controlling false signals among correlated noise variables is an important issue.




