\section{Application to Real Data}
In the real data, two real datasets are considered to evaluate the practical performance of the proposed method. The diamond price dataset has a relatively small number of parameters but contains stronger correlations among the explanatory variables. On the other hand, the house price dataset involves a larger number of predictors and is used to examine the performance of the proposed method in a higher-dimensional setting.

\subsection{Diamond Price Data}
The first real dataset used in this study is the Diamonds dataset from Kaggle. The response variable is \textbf{diamond price}, while the predictors include both numerical variables, such as \textbf{carat}, \textbf{depth}, \textbf{table}, \textbf{x}, \textbf{y}, and \textbf{z}, and categorical variables, such as \textbf{cut}, \textbf{color}, and \textbf{clarity}. The categorical variables are converted into dummy variables before model fitting. To evaluate the predictive performance of the two models, repeated train-test validation is conducted. In each repetition, 20\% of the observations are randomly selected as the training set, and the remaining 20\% are used as the test set. The performance measures include mean squared error (MSE), root mean squared error (RMSE), mean absolute error (MAE), and $R^2$.

The prediction results show that both the original TPBN-MBSP model and the TPBN II model have strong predictive ability. The two models therefore achieve very similar predictive performance. Although the TPBN II model does not produce a lower test MSE than the original TPBN-MBSP model in this analysis, the difference between the two model is small. This suggests that the TPBN II model can incorporate a more complex prior covariance structure while still maintaining competitive predictive performance.

The correlation matrix provides further insight into the structure of the data. The variable \textbf{carat} has the strongest marginal correlation with \textbf{price}, with a correlation of approximately 0.9216. The size-related variables \textbf{x}, \textbf{y}, and \textbf{z} are also highly correlated with \textbf{price}, with correlations of approximately 0.8844, 0.8654, and 0.8612, respectively. More importantly, these size-related predictors are highly correlated with each other. This indicates that the the data contain strong multicollinearity among the predictors related to diamond size.

This high correlation structure provides a suitable setting for evaluating the TPBN II model. The original TPBN-MBSP model mainly controls shrinkage through independent local shrinkage parameters. In contrast, the TPBN II model incorporates predictor dependence by introducing a Laplacian-adjusted covariance structure in the prior distribution of the regression coefficients. Specifically, the prior covariance matrix is modified to include information from the correlation structure of the predictors. Therefore, the TPBN II model is expected to be more flexible in situations where predictors are strongly correlated.

The posterior credible intervals also show that both models identify similar important predictors. On the standardized scale, \textbf{carat} is the dominant positive predictor in both models. The estimated coefficient of \textbf{carat} is approximately 1.3458 under the TPBN-MBSP model, with a 95\% credible interval of approximately $(1.3163, 1.3754)$. Under the TPBN II model, the estimated coefficient approximately 1.3468, with a 95\% credible interval of approximately $(1.3175, 1.3755)$. Since both credible intervals are entirely positive and very similar, the effect of \textbf{carat} on diamond price is stable across the teo methods.

The categorical predictors also show interpretable patterns. The dummy variables corresponding to \textbf{clarity} mostly have positive effects, indicating that diamonds with better \textbf{clarity} levels tend to have higher prices relative to the reference \textbf{clarity} level. For example, \textbf{clarity} levels such as \textbf{IF}, \textbf{WS1}, \textbf{WS2}, \textbf{VS1}, and \textbf{VS2} all have positive estimated effects, and their credible intervals are clearly above zero. In contrast, the dummy variables corresponding to \textbf{color}, are mostly negative, especially for lower color grades such as \textbf{H}, \textbf{I}, and \textbf{J}. This indicates that, relative to the reference \textbf{color} level, these color grades are associated with lower prices. The \textbf{cut} dummy variables are generally positive, suggesting that better \textbf{cut} quality is associated with higher diamond prices.

The variable selection results from repeated cross-validation further show that the two models select largely similar predictors. Both models consistency select important variables such as \textbf{carat}, most clarity dummy variables, several color dummy variables, and cut-related dummy variables. These variables have high selection rates, meaning that their 95\% credible intervals exclude zero in most or all cross-validation splits. This indicates that their effects are stable and not highly sensitive to the particular train-test split.

However, the TPBN II model shows some advantages in terms of selection stability for several predictors. For example, the selection rate of \textbf{colorE} increases from 0.8 under the TPBN-MBSP model to 1.0 under the TPBN II model. Similarly, the selection rate of \textbf{cutGood} increases from 0.9 to 1.0, the selection rate of \textbf{table} increases from 0.7 to 0.9, and the selection rate of \textbf{y} increases from 0.5 to 0.7. These results suggest that the TPBN II model provides more stable variable selection for some predictors whose effects are relatively weak or sensitive to data splitting.

The advantage of the TPBN II model is therefore not mainly reflected in a large reduction in prediction error. Instead, its main contribution is that it incorporates the dependence structure among predictors through the Laplacian-adjusted prior covariance while maintaining predictive performance comparable to that of the original TPBN-MBSP model. This is particularly meaningful for the diamond data, where several predictors, especially \textbf{carat}, \textbf{x}, \textbf{y}, and \textbf{z}, are highly correlated. By accounting for this correlation structure, the TPBN II model provides a more structurally flexible prior specification and improves the stability of variable selection for some predictors.

In summary, both the TPBN-MBSP and the TPBN II model perform well in predicting diamond prices. The TPBN-MBSP model achieves a slightly lower test MSE in this analysis, but the difference between the two models is small. The TPBN II model remains competitive in terms of prediction accuracy and provides an additional advantage by incorporating predictor correlation information into the prior covariance structure. Under strong multicollinearity, this structural flexibility may lead to more stable coefficient estimation and variable selection. Therefore, the TPBN II model can be viewed as a useful extension of the original TPBN-MBSP framework for regression problems involving highly correlated predictors.

