\subsection{House Price Data}
The second real dataset used in this study is the house price datasets also from Kaggle. This dataset contains residential housing information from Ames, Iowa, and includes 79 explanatory variables describing different aspects of each house, such as building characteristics, location-related information, lot size, basement quality, garage features, and other housing attributes. In this analysis, the response variable is SalePrice, which represents the final sale price of each house. The proposed method and the original TPBN-MBSP method were compared using 10 repeated corss-validation experiments, where 20\% of the observations were used as training data and 80\% were used as testing data. The results show that proposed method achieved substantially better fitting performace on the training data. Specifically, proposed method produced a lower training RMSE, lower MAE, and higher $R^2$ than TPBN-MBSP, indicating that the proposed method was able to capture more information from the training samples.

However, TPBN-MBSP showed better out of sample prediction performance on the testing data. The test RMSE and MAE of TPBN-MBSP were lower than those of proposed method and the test $R^2$ of TPBN-MBSP was also higher. This suggests that although proposed method has stronger fitting ability, it may be more sensitive to the training samples under the current cross-validation setting. Since only 20\% of the observations were used for training, the estimation of a more flexible correlation-aware structure may become unstable, leading to a larger variation in test performance.

Despite this limitation, proposed method provides an important methodological advantage over TPBN-MBSP. The TPBN-MBSP method mainly applies shrinkage to individual regression coefficients, while proposed method further incorporates the correlation structure among predictors through the modified prior covariance structure. This is particularly useful for house price data, where many predictors are highly correlated. For example, variables such as \textbf{garage data}, \textbf{number of garage cars}, \textbf{living area}, \textbf{total rooms}, \textbf{basement area}, and \textbf{first-floor} exhibit strong correlations. By incorporating such structural information, proposed method can identify a broader set of related predictors and provide a more flexible framework for modeling correlated features.

Under the criterion of selection rate greater than or equal to 0.3, the TPBN-MBSP method identified 13 candidate predictors, among which 11 predictors were finally selected based on the 95\% credible interval criterion. In contrast, the proposed method identified 49 candidate predictors with selection rates greater than or equal to 0.3, but only 9 predictors were finally selected after applying the credible interval criterion. This result suggests that the proposed method is able to explore a broader set of potentially relevant predictors across repeated data splits, which may be attributed to its incorporation of the correlation structure among regression coefficients. However, the final selected set remains relatively sparse because the credible interval criterion only retains predictors with stable nonzero effects. Therefore, the proposed method does not simply include more variables in the model; instead, it provides a more correlation-aware screening process while maintaining conservative and stable final variable selection.

In summary, TPBN-MBSP performs better in terms of testing prediction accuracy in the current experiment, while proposed method demonstrates stronger training fit and greater ability to reflect the dependence structure among predictors. Therefore, proposed method can be viewed as a useful extension of TPBN-MBSP for correlated high-dimensional regression settings, although further tuning or stronger regularization may be required to improve its out-of-sample prediction stability.



