\section{Conclusion and Discussion}

The central idea of this thesis is to capture covariance information among regression coefficients by modifying the prior structure. In the original TPBN-MBSP framework, the prior mainly relies on global-local shrinkage to induce sparsity, and the regression coefficients are treated through independent local shrinkage components. Although this structure is effective for high-dimensional sparse regression, it does not explicitly use the correlation information among predictors. When predictors are highly correlated, this limitation may lead to unstable coefficient estimation, arbitrary variable selection within correlated groups, or excessive shrinkage of some truly important variables. Therefore, this thesis proposes a new method that extends the TPBN-MBSP framework by incorporating the predictor correlation structure into the prior covariance matrix.

The simulation studies show that the relative performance of the two methods depends strongly on the correlation structure of the predictors. When the predictors are independent or only moderately correlated, the original TPBN-MBSP method often performs better in estimating individual nonzero coefficients. This result is reasonable because, in such settings, there is little correlation structure for the proposed method to exploit, and the additional covariance component may introduce unnecessary flexibility or stronger regularization. However, as the correlation among predictors becomes stronger, the proposed method begins to show its advantage. In particular, under high or extremely high correlation, TPBN-MBSP tends to over-shrink some truly nonzero coefficients or produce unstable estimates for correlated noise variables. In contrast, the proposed method is more capable of stabilizing estimation by borrowing information across correlated predictors. This advantage is especially clear in the high-dimensional setting, where the proposed method reduces false signals among correlated irrelevant predictors and controls posterior uncertainty more effectively than TPBN-MBSP.

The real data analyses further demonstrate the practical meaning of the proposed method. In the diamond price data, both TPBN-MBSP and the proposed method achieve similar predictive performance. Although the proposed method does not substantially reduce the prediction error, it maintains competitive prediction accuracy while incorporating a more flexible prior covariance structure. In the higher dimensional data, house price data, the proposed method shows stronger fitting ability on the training data, while TPBN-MBSP performs better on the testing data. This indicates that the proposed method may be more flexible but also more sensitive to the training sample, especially when the training proportion is small and the number of predictors is relatively large. Nevertheless, the proposed method identifies a broader set of potentially relevant predictors during repeated cross-validation and then applies the credible interval criterion to retain a sparse final selected set. This behavior suggests that the proposed method does not simply select more variables, but rather provides a correlation-aware screening process before final variable selection.

Overall, the proposed method can be viewed as a structured extension of TPBN-MBSP for high-dimensional regression with highly correlated predictors. Its main contribution is not necessarily a universal improvement in prediction error, but the introduction of a prior covariance structure that explicitly reflects predictor dependence. The simulation and real data results suggest that this structural modification is particularly useful when the data contain strong correlation or near-collinearity among predictors. In such cases, the proposed method can improve estimation stability, reduce false signals among correlated noise variables, and provide a more interpretable Bayesian shrinkage framework.

Several limitations remain for future work. First, the tuning parameter \(\phi\), which controls the strength of the Laplacian component, is fixed in this thesis. A data-adaptive or fully Bayesian treatment of \(\phi\) may improve the balance between sparsity and correlation-aware smoothing. Second, the correlation threshold used to construct the predictor graph may affect the final Laplacian structure, and more systematic threshold selection procedures could be considered. Third, the proposed method may require stronger regularization or more stable tuning strategies in real high-dimensional applications to improve out-of-sample prediction performance. Finally, future studies may extend the current univariate response formulation to the full multivariate TPBN-MBSP setting, where correlations among both predictors and responses can be jointly modeled. These extensions would further strengthen the applicability of the proposed framework to complex high-dimensional and highly correlated data.



